-- title:
--   Initial form of g ∈ (X₀,X₁)ᵈ under a ring map
-- statement:
--   Let $W$ be a commutative ring, $M$ a local ring with maximal ideal $\mathfrak m_M$, and let $\Phi \colon W[[X_0,X_1]] \to M$ and $\rho \colon W \to M$ be ring homomorphisms such that $\Phi$ agrees with $\rho$ on constants, i.e. $\Phi(C(w)) = \rho(w)$ for all $w \in W$. Suppose given $v, \alpha, \beta \in M$ and $p_0, p_1 \in W$ with $\Phi(X_0) = v\alpha$ and $\Phi(X_1) = v\beta$, with $v \in \mathfrak m_M$, and with $\alpha - \rho(p_0) \in \mathfrak m_M$ and $\beta - \rho(p_1) \in \mathfrak m_M$. Then for every natural number $d$ and every $g$ lying in the $d$-th power of the ideal of $W[[X_0,X_1]]$ generated by $X_0$ and $X_1$, there exists $r \in \mathfrak m_M$ with $$\Phi(g) = v^{d}\Bigl(\rho\Bigl(\sum_{i=0}^{d} g_{(i,\,d-i)}\, p_0^{\,i} p_1^{\,d-i}\Bigr) + r\Bigr),$$ where $g_{(i,\,d-i)}$ denotes the coefficient of $g$ at the exponent vector sending $0 \mapsto i$ and $1 \mapsto d-i$, and the sum runs over $i \in \{0,1,\dots,d\}$. Thus $\Phi(g)$ is $v^d$ times the value, under $\rho$, of the degree-$d$ initial form of $g$ evaluated at the direction $(p_0 : p_1)$, up to an error in $\mathfrak m_M$.
--
--   This is the algebraic reading of an initial form (lowest-degree homogeneous part) along a tangent direction: a homomorphism out of a two-variable power series ring which sends both variables into the principal ideal generated by $v$ detects, modulo the maximal ideal, only the degree-$d$ form of $g$ evaluated at the specified direction. It is used in the analysis of germs at the ends of the blow-up of the supersingular points on modular curves, where both variables map to $v$ times an element congruent to a rational direction.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_MvPowerSeries_exists_apply_eq_mul_pow_mul_add_of_mem_span_X_pow_of_apply_X_eq_mul.lean

